{
  "dom_id": "step-3973",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "Recall that the absolute value of a number represents the distance of that number from the origin.\n\nLet's take a look at the number line below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14157906/assets/e20db43f722fc4db42fc845ab20a7eef2c06437cc1d2246e0aed47dc01107aba.png)\n\nSince $-3$ and $3$ are at the same distance from the origin, we have\n\n$$\n|-3| = |3| = 3.\n$$\n\nIn general, for any real number $a,$\n\n$$\n|-a| = |a|.\n$$\n\nWe can use this fact to simplify expressions containing absolute value. Let's see an example.",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
